<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Love song · 1905 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Amar Sonar Bangla</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="bn">আমার সোনার বাংলা</bdi> / <bdi lang="bn-Latn">Amar Sonar Bangla</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song by Rabindranath Tagore in praise of the land of Bengal that became the national anthem of Bangladesh in 1971.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Rabindranath Tagore</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Rabindranath Tagore, after a melody of Gagan Harkara</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bengalis; Bangladesh</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bengal (present-day India and Bangladesh)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first published September 1905</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Rabindranath Tagore (first published 1905))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a>&quot; (&quot;My Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>&quot;) is an ode to Mother Bengal written by the Bengali poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>. The exact year it was composed is not known, because the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was never found. Its melody is derived from &quot;Ami Kothay Pabo Tare&quot;, a song of the Baul singer Gagan Harkara, set to the rhythm cycle Dadra tala.</p><p>It is linked with the first Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In 1905 the British authorities split the province of the Bengal Presidency in two: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>, announced the decision on 20 July, and it took effect on 16 October. The division ran along communal lines, with a Muslim majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, and it is claimed to have been politically motivated. With many other songs, this one was meant to revive the unified spirit of Bengal and raise public feeling against the divide. Tagore's biographer Prashanta Kumar Paul held that it was first sung on 25 August 1905 at a gathering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. The words first appeared in print in the September 1905 issues of the periodicals Bongodorshon and Baul, and the song with its musical notation appeared the same month in the music journal Shongeet Biggnan Praveshika. The notation was taken down by Tagore's niece Indira Devi as she heard him sing it.</p><p>In 1971, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>, the Provisional Government of Bangladesh adopted it as the national anthem; the first ten lines of the poem are the version usually sung. The orchestral rendition was composed by Samar Das. When the Constitution of Bangladesh was drafted in 1972, it was chosen over &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-dhanya-pushpa-bhara">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</a>&quot; by Dwijendra Lal Roy. Syed Ali Ahsan translated it into English.</p><p>It has faced criticism since at least 1975. Opponents have argued that it was written against a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, the territory that became Bangladesh, that its author was not a Bangladeshi, and that the mother it addresses is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess. After the coup of 15 August 1975, President Khondaker Mostaq Ahmed set up a committee to replace it, which recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazi-nazrul-islam">Kazi Nazrul Islam</a>'s &quot;Notuner Gaan&quot; (today the national march) and Farrukh Ahmed's &quot;Panjeri&quot;; the process stopped when he was removed. In 1979 Prime Minister Shah Azizur Rahman proposed Shahnaz Rahmatullah's &quot;Prothom Bangladesh&quot; instead, and under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman">Ziaur Rahman</a> that song was played after the anthem on national television and at government events until his death in 1981. In 2002 the minister Motiur Rahman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> proposed changing its words, which the Cabinet Division did not accept. Calls to replace it were made again in 2024 and 2025.</p><p>On 26 March 2014, Bangladesh's independence day, 254,537 people sang it together at the National Parade Ground in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, a world record for the most people singing a national anthem at once, approved by Guinness World Records on 9 April 2014; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> broke the record on 21 January 2017.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of love for the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, addressed to it as a beloved mother and praising how precious it is. The mood is tender and devoted.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Bengali</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Bengali</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">আমার সোনার বাংলা, আমি তোমায় ভালোবাসি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">āmāra sonāra bāṁlā, āmi tomāẏa bhālobāsi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My golden Bengal, I love you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">চিরদিন তোমার আকাশ, তোমার বাতাস, আমার প্রাণে বাজায় বাঁশি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">ciradina tomāra ākāśa, tomāra bātāsa, āmāra prāṇe bājāẏa bām̐śi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Forever your sky, your air play a flute in my heart.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা, ফাগুনে তোর আমের বনে ঘ্রাণে পাগল করে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā, phāgune tora āmera bane ghrāṇe pāgala kare,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, in Phalgun the scent of your mango groves drives me mad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মরি হায়, হায় রে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mari hāẏa, hāẏa re—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, I could die, ah, ah—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা, অঘ্রানে তোর ভরা ক্ষেতে আমি কী দেখেছি মধুর হাসি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā, aghrāne tora bharā kṣete āmi kī dekhechi madhura hāsi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, in Aghran, in your full fields, what a sweet smile I have seen!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">কী শোভা, কী ছায়া গো, কী স্নেহ, কী মায়া গো—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">kī śobhā, kī chāẏā go, kī sneha, kī māẏā go—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What beauty, what shade, what tenderness, what love—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">কী আঁচল বিছায়েছ বটের মূলে, নদীর কূলে কূলে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">kī ām̐cala bichāẏecha baṭera mūle, nadīra kūle kūle.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what a sari's end you have spread at the foot of the banyan, along the banks of the rivers!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মা, তোর মুখের বাণী আমার কানে লাগে সুধার মতো,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mā, tora mukhera bāṇī āmāra kāne lāge sudhāra mato,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, the words of your mouth touch my ears like nectar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মরি হায়, হায় রে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mari hāẏa, hāẏa re—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, I could die, ah, ah—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মা, তোর বদনখানি মলিন হলে, ও মা, আমি নয়নজলে ভাসি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mā, tora badanakhāni malina hale, o mā, āmi naẏanajale bhāsi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, when your face grows dim, O Mother, I float in the water of my eyes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তোমার এই খেলাঘরে শিশুকাল কাটিল রে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tomāra ei khelāghare śiśukāla kāṭila re,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this playhouse of yours my childhood passed;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তোমারি ধুলামাটি অঙ্গে মাখি ধন্য জীবন মানি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tomāri dhulāmāṭi aṅge mākhi dhanya jībana māni.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>smearing your own dust and earth on my limbs, I count my life blessed.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তুই দিন ফুরালে সন্ধ্যাকালে কী দীপ জ্বালিস ঘরে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tui dina phurāle sandhyākāle kī dīpa jbālisa ghare,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the day runs out, at evening, what a lamp you light in the house!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মরি হায়, হায় রে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mari hāẏa, hāẏa re—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, I could die, ah, ah—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তখন খেলাধুলা সকল ফেলে, ও মা, তোমার কোলে ছুটে আসি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">takhana khelādhulā sakala phele, o mā, tomāra kole chuṭe āsi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then, leaving all my play, O Mother, I come running to your lap.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ধেনু-চরা তোমার মাঠে, পারে যাবার খেয়াঘাটে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">dhenu-carā tomāra māṭhe, pāre yābāra kheẏāghāṭe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In your fields where the cattle graze, at the ferry landings for crossing over,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সারা দিন পাখি-ডাকা ছায়ায়-ঢাকা তোমার পল্লীবাটে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">sārā dina pākhi-ḍākā chāẏāẏa-ḍhākā tomāra pallībāṭe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on your village paths loud with birds all day and covered in shade,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তোমার ধানে-ভরা আঙিনাতে জীবনের দিন কাটে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tomāra dhāne-bharā āṅināte jībanera dina kāṭe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in your courtyards full of rice the days of life pass,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মরি হায়, হায় রে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mari hāẏa, hāẏa re—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, I could die, ah, ah—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা, আমার যে ভাই তারা সবাই, ও মা, তোমার রাখাল তোমার চাষি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā, āmāra ye bhāi tārā sabāi, o mā, tomāra rākhāla tomāra cāṣi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, those who are my brothers, all of them, O Mother, are your herdsmen, your farmers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা, তোর চরণেতে দিলেম এই মাথা পেতে–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā, tora caraṇete dilema ei māthā pete–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, at your feet I have laid down this head—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">দে গো তোর পায়ের ধূলা, সে যে আমার মাথার মানিক হবে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">de go tora pāẏera dhūlā, se ye āmāra māthāra mānika habe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give me the dust of your feet: it will be the jewel on my head.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা, গরিবের ধন যা আছে তাই দিব চরণতলে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā, garibera dhana yā āche tāi diba caraṇatale,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, what wealth the poor have, that I will give at your feet;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মরি হায়, হায় রে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mari hāẏa, hāẏa re—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, I could die, ah, ah—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">আমি পরের ঘরে কিনব না আর, মা, তোর ভূষণ ব’লে গলার ফাঁসি॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">āmi parera ghare kinaba nā āra, mā, tora bhūṣaṇa ba’le galāra phām̐si.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will no longer buy in others' houses, Mother, calling it your ornament, a noose for my neck.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Rabindranath Tagore (1905); Gitabitan, Swadesh 1. Text from <a href="https://bn.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A6%97%E0%A7%80%E0%A6%A4%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%A4%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A8%2F%E0%A6%B8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%B6%2F%E0%A7%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>The romanization is a strict transliteration of the Bengali letters (the inherent vowel written as 'a', as in the site's Jana Gana Mana): 'āmāra' is sung āmār, 'bāṁlā' Bāṅlā.</li><li>line 1.3 and 1.5: Phalgun (Phāgun) is the spring month of February to March; Aghran (Agrahāyaṇ) the harvest month of November to December.</li><li>lines x.4: 'mari hāẏa', literally 'I die, alas', is an exclamation of overwhelming feeling, here of love and wonder.</li><li>line 2.2: 'ām̐cala', the loose end of a sari, spread out as a mother spreads it to shelter or seat a child.</li><li>line 2.5: 'badanakhāni malina hale', literally 'when your face becomes soiled or dim', that is, sad.</li><li>The singer addresses Bengal both as 'tumi' (your: tomāra) and with the intimate 'tui' (your: tora), as the original alternates.</li><li>line 5.5: 'pare', others: the foreign goods of the Swadeshi boycott of 1905; the 'ornament' bought from others is a noose.</li></ul></details></section>
